ALSA (Advanced Linux Sound Architecture) is the kernel-level sound
system. Even with PipeWire, ALSA provides the low-level device access.

@code bash
# Install ALSA utilities
sudo pacman -S alsa-utils

@text
alsamixer is the ncurses-based mixer for interactive volume control.

@code bash
# Open interactive mixer
alsamixer                        # default card
alsamixer -c 0                   # specific card number
alsamixer -c PCH                 # by card name

# Navigation inside alsamixer:
#   Left/Right  - select control
#   Up/Down     - adjust volume
#   M           - mute/unmute
#   F3          - playback controls
#   F4          - capture controls
#   F5          - all controls
#   Esc/Q       - quit

@text
amixer is the command-line mixer for scripting and keybindings.

@code bash
# Show all controls
amixer
amixer -c 0                      # specific card
amixer scontrols                 # list simple mixer controls
amixer scontents                 # show all controls with values

# Get/set volume
amixer get Master
amixer set Master 80%            # set to 80%
amixer set Master 5%+            # increase by 5%
amixer set Master 5%-            # decrease by 5%

# Mute/unmute
amixer set Master mute
amixer set Master unmute
amixer set Master toggle

# Capture (microphone) controls
amixer set Capture 80%
amixer set Capture cap            # enable capture
amixer set Capture nocap          # disable capture

@text
aplay and arecord handle PCM playback and recording at the ALSA level.

@code bash
# List sound cards and devices
aplay -l                         # list playback devices
aplay -L                         # list PCM devices (with aliases)
arecord -l                       # list capture devices

# Play audio
aplay file.wav                   # play WAV file
aplay -D hw:0,0 file.wav        # specific hardware device
aplay -D default file.wav       # default device

# Record audio
arecord -f cd output.wav         # CD quality (16-bit, 44.1kHz, stereo)
arecord -d 10 output.wav        # record for 10 seconds
arecord -f S16_LE -r 48000 -c 2 output.wav   # explicit format
arecord -vv /dev/null            # VU meter (test microphone levels)

# List supported formats
aplay --dump-hw-params -D hw:0,0 /dev/null

@text
Speaker test generates test tones to verify output channels.

@code bash
# Test speakers
speaker-test                     # default: front left/right
speaker-test -c 2                # stereo
speaker-test -c 6                # 5.1 surround
speaker-test -t wav              # use WAV samples instead of pink noise
speaker-test -D hw:0,0           # specific device

@text
alsactl saves and restores mixer state.

@code bash
# Save current mixer state
sudo alsactl store               # saves to /var/lib/alsa/asound.state

# Restore mixer state
sudo alsactl restore

# Initialize cards to default
sudo alsactl init

# OpenRC service handles save/restore at boot/shutdown
sudo rc-update add alsasound boot

@text
ALSA configuration files for persistent settings.

@code bash
# System-wide: /etc/asound.conf
# Per-user: ~/.asoundrc

# Set default card (in ~/.asoundrc)
# defaults.pcm.card 1
# defaults.ctl.card 1

# Or using named device:
# pcm.!default {
#     type hw
#     card PCH
# }
# ctl.!default {
#     type hw
#     card PCH
# }

# List card names and numbers
cat /proc/asound/cards
